// =============================================================================
//  probe_sim.hml — W4-9's acceptance harness.   ARCHITECTURE §3, §4.3, §5.4.
// =============================================================================
//
//      hemlockc -O1 tools/probe_sim.hml -o /tmp/probe_sim
//      /tmp/probe_sim                     # compiled — the shipping path
//      hemlock  tools/probe_sim.hml       # interpreted — parity
//      nm /tmp/probe_sim | grep -c SDL_   # MUST be 0
//
//  Exits 1 on any failed assertion, and asserts its own assertion COUNT so a
//  section that silently stops running is a failure rather than a green run.
//
//  BUILD_PLAN W4-9 says "verified by `simtest`, `replay`, `benchframe`".  Those
//  three are W2-13's and W4-13's files and several agents are in this tree, so
//  this probe is a SEPARATE tool rather than an edit to shared ones (CLAUDE.md
//  §3: edit the files your task lists and no others).  Folding it in at the
//  wave gate is a one-line call; the assertions are the same either way.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WHY THIS FILE IMPORTS `src/render/render_snapshot.hml`
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  W4-9's brief is "emit two snapshots so the renderer can interpolate between
//  them; rendering must never be locked to the tick rate".  A probe that only
//  checked the sim side would be checking half a seam.  `tools/*.hml` are NOT
//  walled by `ci_imports.sh` (they are the things that prove the wall), so this
//  file drives the REAL renderer-side consumer against the REAL sim and asserts
//  the two endpoints agree bit-for-bit.  It links no SDL, because
//  `render_snapshot.hml` imports none.
// =============================================================================

